PILOT_047:LINK1:K047S01,PILOT_047:LINK1,K047S01,https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/high-blood-pressure/symptoms,proposed_new_source,needs_more_evidence,Undiagnosed or uncontrolled high blood pressure,Candidate evidence concerning hypertension and vascular complications,supports,High Blood Pressure - Symptoms → Learn About the Effects of High Blood Pressure video description; Undiagnosed or uncontrolled high blood pressure can lead to serious health problems,Persistent or uncontrolled high blood pressure can damage arterial walls over time. NHLBI explicitly describes hypertension-related arterial wall damage and identifies stroke and atherosclerosis among the serious complications of uncontrolled high blood pressure.,CAUSES,The explicit arterial-wall damage statement appears in the video description rather than a detailed written mechanism. The page does not specify cerebral arterial injury or establish a complete pathway from hypertension to stroke. Additional mechanism-specific evidence is required for LINK2.,STU_001,2026-11-14,Reviewed NHLBI High Blood Pressure - Symptoms. The video description explicitly states that high blood pressure damages artery walls over time. The complications section identifies stroke and atherosclerosis as consequences of uncontrolled hypertension. This supports PILOT_047:LINK1 (Persistent high blood pressure → Arterial wall damage). The source does not independently establish the intermediate mechanism leading to stroke. AI-assisted review; human confirmation pending.
PILOT_047:LINK2:K047S02,PILOT_047:LINK2,K047S02,https://www.cdc.gov/stroke/risk-factors/index.html,proposed_new_source,needs_more_evidence,Conditions that can increase risk > High blood pressure,Candidate evidence for hypertension and stroke risk; intermediate vascular injury not established,supports,Stroke and high blood pressure; What happens when you have a stroke,"Persistent high blood pressure damages arterial walls and increases the likelihood of arterial blockage or rupture. Damaged cerebral arteries can become narrowed or blocked, contributing to ischemic stroke, or rupture, resulting in hemorrhagic stroke.",RISK_FACTOR_FOR,"Arterial damage increases stroke risk but does not inevitably cause stroke. Ischemic stroke involves interrupted blood flow, whereas hemorrhagic stroke involves vessel rupture. The source supports both mechanisms but does not quantify the probability of either pathway or establish a specific vascular lesion in an individual patient.",STU_001,2026-11-14,"Verified American Stroke Association ""How High Blood Pressure Can Lead to Stroke,"" specifically ""Stroke and high blood pressure"" and ""What happens when you have a stroke."" The source explicitly explains that hypertension damages arteries and makes them more susceptible to blockage or rupture. Blocked cerebral vessels can cause ischemic stroke, while ruptured cerebral vessels can cause hemorrhagic stroke. Both mechanisms support Arterial wall damage → Increased stroke risk. AI-assisted verification; human confirmation pending."
